<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ichadon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">501–<a href="/years/527">527</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ichadon was a court official of Silla whose martyrdom in 527 led to the kingdom's acceptance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. According to tradition he offered to be executed to overcome the opposition of the nobles to the new faith, and when he was beheaded milk-white blood flowed from his neck. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beopheung-of-silla">King Beopheung</a> then made Buddhism the official religion of Silla.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ichadon was also known as Kŏch'adon and by the courtesy names Yŏmch'ok and Yŏmdo. He is remembered as a Buddhist monk and an adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beopheung-of-silla">King Beopheung</a>, who in one account calls him his Grand Secretary. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-yusa">Samguk yusa</a> his family name was Pak, and his mother's family descended from the father of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jijeung-of-silla">Jijeung of Silla</a>. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was recognised, a form of shamanism that treated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bak-hyeokgeose">Bak Hyeokgeose</a> as a god was widespread, and Beopheung is said to have chosen Ichadon, a descendant of Hyeokgeose, to establish the new faith.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-yusa">Samguk yusa</a> tells his story as a tale of miracle. Early in his reign Beopheung wished to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> the state religion, but his officials opposed it. In the fourteenth year of the reign Ichadon devised a plan: the king would issue a proclamation under the royal seal granting Buddhism official sanction, and when the opposing officials demanded an explanation he would deny having made it. Ichadon would then confess to what would be taken for a forgery and accept execution, and he foretold that a wonder at his death would show the officials the power of the faith.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The plan went as arranged, and he was executed on the 15th day of the 9th month in 527. In the tale the earth shook, the sun darkened, flowers rained from the sky, his severed head flew to the sacred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geum-river">Geumgang</a> mountains and milk sprayed 100 feet into the air from his body. The opposing officials accepted the omen as a sign of heaven's approval. His body was taken to the Geumgang mountains and buried there with respect, and his martyrdom led to the building of Heungnyunsa, Silla's first state-sponsored temple.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ichadon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>King Beopheung recognised Buddhism in 527 after the martyrdom of the courtier <b>Ichadon</b>, and it became the faith of court and aristocracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/silla">Silla · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
